reasoning:
We need to analyze the failure.

The journey steps:

Step 0: seek #store

Step 1: type "ma"

Assertion expects #autoCompleteJS_result_1 value "Marina Park".

Step 2: press ArrowDown -> assertions:
- attribute aria-selected on #autoCompleteJS_result_0 true
- attribute aria-activedescendant on #store equals autoCompleteJS_result_0

But observed shows after pressing ArrowDown, aria-activedescendant was "Market Street", which corresponds to result 0? Wait they expected result 0 but got Market Street? Actually they typed "ma" then suggestions likely filtered: Marina Park, Maple Avenue maybe? Let's see data: ["Market Street","Marina Park","Maple Avenue","Harbour Mall","Station Square"]. Typing "ma" yields matches: "Marina Park", "Maple Avenue"? Also "Market Street" contains "ma"? Yes "Market Street" contains "ma" as substring? "Market" starts with "Mar"? Actually "Market" contains "ar". Not "ma". But case-insensitive lowercasing: "market street". Contains "ma"? "market" has "ma" at positions? "market": m a r k e t -> yes "ma" at start. So "Market Street" also matches. So suggestions likely: Market Street, Marina Park, Maple Avenue (maybe Harbour Mall? contains "ma"? Harbour has "ar"? Not). So three suggestions.

Thus after typing "ma", the list shows three items: Market Street (index0), Marina Park (index1), Maple Avenue (index2). The test expects after first ArrowDown, the first suggestion (index0) should be highlighted, i.e., aria-activedescendant should point to result0 (Market Street). However the observed aria-activedescendant was "Market Street"? Wait they said observed: aria-activedescendant="Market Street". That seems correct? But assertion expected value "autoCompleteJS_result_0". They expect the attribute value to be the ID of the element, not its text content. Indeed aria-activedescendant should refer to the ID of the option element, like "autoCompleteJS_result_0". In the observed HTML snippet we saw: aria-activedescendant="Market Street". That's wrong: they set it to the text content instead of the ID.

Thus bug: In navigationController.js, when setting aria-activedescendant, they set it to list[currentFocus].dataset.value. dataset.value is set in Item.js: result.setAttribute("data-value", item.value[item.key]); For each item, item.value is the original object (like {name: "Market Street"}), item.key is "name". So dataset.value becomes the name string, not the ID. Should be the ID attribute of the element, which is set as `${config.resultItem.className}_${index}` (e.g., "autoCompleteJS_result_0").

Thus fix: In navigationController.js, within update function, after adding active class, set aria-activedescendant to list[currentFocus].id (or getAttribute('id')). Currently they set to dataset.value.

Let's look at navigationController.js source:

```
  const update = (event, list, state, config) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    if (state) {
      // If the arrow DOWN or TAB key is pressed
      // increase the currentFocus
      currentFocus++;
    } else {
      // If the arrow UP or TAB key is pressed
      // decrease the currentFocus
      currentFocus--;
    }
    // and add \"active\" class to the list item
    addActive(list);
    config.inputField.setAttribute(\"aria-activedescendant\", list[currentFocus].dataset.value);
    /** 
     * @emits {navigation} Emits Event on results list navigation
     **/
    eventEmitter(
      event.srcElement,
      { event, ...dataFeedback, matches: results, selection: results[currentFocus] },
      \"navigation\"
    );
  };
```

Need to change `.dataset.value` to `.id`.

Also ensure that the ID is set correctly: In Item.js, they set id: `${config.resultItem.className}_${index}`. The className is "autoCompleteJS_result". So id becomes "autoCompleteJS_result_0", etc. Good.

